To capture and inspect a Wi-Fi login page (captive portal) on Ubuntu, you need to monitor the network traffic at the exact moment your device connects and attempts to communicate with the internet.
Here is the complete step-by-step process to capture the login page redirect using Wireshark (for full packet analysis) and Browser Developer Tools (for inspecting the webpage itself).

Method 1: Using Wireshark (Full Network Packet Capture)

This method captures the raw network packets to show exactly how the router intercepts your connection and redirects you to the login page.

Step 1: Install Wireshark

Open your terminal (Ctrl + Alt + T) and run the following commands to install Wireshark and configure it so you don't have to run it as root (which is safer):
bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install wireshark -y
Use code with caution.
During installation, a prompt will ask if non-superusers should be able to capture packets. Select Yes.
To apply this change to your current user, add yourself to the wireshark group:
bash
sudo usermod -aG wireshark $USER
Use code with caution.
(Note: You may need to log out and log back into Ubuntu for the group change to take effect).

Step 2: Identify Your Wireless Interface Name

In the terminal, find the name of your Wi-Fi card by running:
bash
ip link
Use code with caution.
Look for an interface that starts with wlan or wlp (for example, wlp3s0). Note this name down.

Step 3: Start Wireshark and Begin Capturing

1. Open Wireshark from your Ubuntu applications menu.
2. In the list of interfaces on the home screen, double-click your Wi-Fi interface (e.g., wlp3s0).
3. Wireshark will immediately start capturing all network traffic in real-time.

Step 4: Trigger the Capture

1. Disconnect from your current Wi-Fi and connect to the target Wi-Fi network that requires the password/login page.
2. Open a web browser and try to navigate to a plain, unencrypted website like http://neverssl.com.
	• Why? Modern HTTPS websites encrypt traffic, making it harder for the router to cleanly redirect you. An HTTP site forces the router to step in and send the login page.

Step 5: Filter for the Login Page Redirection

Go back to Wireshark, click the red square icon in the top left to Stop the capture, and type this filter into the top bar:
text
http.response.code == 302 || http.response.code == 307
Use code with caution.
Press Enter. Look at the Info column. You will see an HTTP packet indicating a redirect. Click on it, expand the Hypertext Transfer Protocol section in the bottom pane, and look for the Location: line. This line contains the exact URL of the Wi-Fi login page.

Method 2: Using Browser Developer Tools (Web Layer Capture)

If you just want to capture the webpage code, its forms, and the login URL without dealing with raw network packets, your browser has everything built-in.

Step 1: Open Your Browser's Network Monitor

1. Open Firefox or Chrome/Chromium on Ubuntu.
2. Press F12 (or right-click anywhere and select Inspect).
3. Click on the Network tab at the top of the developer panel.

Step 2: Enable "Persist Logs"

This is the most critical step. Usually, when a page redirects, the browser clears the screen.
* In Firefox: Click the gear icon on the right side of the Network tab and check Persist Logs.
* In Chrome: Check the box that says Preserve log near the top left of the Network tab.

Step 3: Trigger the Login Page

1. Connect to the Wi-Fi network.
2. In your browser's URL bar, type http://neverssl.com and hit Enter.
3. The network tab will instantly populate with rows of data.

Step 4: Analyze the Captured Requests

1. Look at the very first row in the Network tab. You will see neverssl.com with a Status code of 302 or 307 (Redirect).
2. Click on that row and look at the Headers tab on the right side.
3. Under Response Headers, locate the Location: field. This displays the web address of the login portal.
4. The rows immediately following it will show the images, HTML files, and scripts that make up the Wi-Fi login screen itself.
Now that you know how to find the URL and headers, are you looking to troubleshoot why a portal isn't opening automatically, or are you looking to analyze how the login page processes credentials?
